## Encapsulation with Private Types

### The Pattern

```xiom
mod cache {
  type CacheData {
    store: Vec[Str];
    max_size: Int;
  }

  pub fn create(max: Int) -> CacheData {
    CacheData{ store: Vec[Str].new(), max_size: max }
  }

  pub fn insert(c: &mut CacheData, item: Str) -> Bool {
    if c.store.len() >= c.max_size {
      return false;
    };
    c.store.push(item);
    true
  }

  pub fn lookup(c: &CacheData, item: Str) -> Bool {
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < c.store.len() {
      if c.store.get(i).unwrap() == item {
        return true;
      };
      i += 1;
    };
    false
  }

  pub fn size(c: &CacheData) -> Int {
    c.store.len()
  }
}
```

### Why This Matters

- `CacheData` fields are hidden — users can't `c.store.push()` directly
- You can change the internal Vec to a Map without breaking any user code
- All mutations go through functions that can validate and protect invariants
- The type is an implementation detail, not a public commitment

### Using It

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  use cache;
  let mut c = cache.create(5);
  cache.insert(&mut c, "key1");
  io.println(cache.lookup(&c, "key1"));
  return 0;
}
```

Users interact through functions only. The type itself is opaque.

